00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:36.207
[MUSIC]

00:00:36.207 --> 00:00:40.647
This podcast is dedicated to
helping people deal with everyday

00:00:40.647 --> 00:00:44.594
business challenges with confidence and
conviction.

00:00:44.594 --> 00:00:47.814
However, today we're going
to take a different tack.

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:52.425
We're going to look at another
challenge that many of us face,

00:00:52.425 --> 00:00:55.037
helping teenagers become adults.

00:00:55.037 --> 00:00:59.748
And the things that we learn in this
conversation will actually help us be

00:00:59.748 --> 00:01:01.458
better in the workplace.

00:01:01.458 --> 00:01:02.807
I'm Matt Abrahams and

00:01:02.807 --> 00:01:07.362
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:01:07.362 --> 00:01:12.038
Welcome to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, the podcast.

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:16.006
I am really looking forward to
speaking with Julie Lythcott-Haims.

00:01:16.006 --> 00:01:19.099
Julie is multi-talented,
a lawyer by training, and

00:01:19.099 --> 00:01:22.947
a former dean of freshmen and
undergraduate advising at Stanford.

00:01:22.947 --> 00:01:27.831
She is now a much sought after speaker and
the author of the award winning book,

00:01:27.831 --> 00:01:31.752
How to Raise an Adult,
Break Free of the Overparenting Trap and

00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:33.686
Prepare Your Kid for Success.

00:01:33.686 --> 00:01:37.985
Julie now has a new book,
Your Turn, How to be an Adult.

00:01:37.985 --> 00:01:39.935
Thanks for being here, Julie.

00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:41.545
&gt;&gt; Matt, thanks so much for having me.

00:01:41.545 --> 00:01:44.264
I'm really looking forward to
communicating with you today.

00:01:44.264 --> 00:01:46.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Excellent, me too.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.397
Let's jump right in.

00:01:48.397 --> 00:01:52.403
Can you define what you
mean by the term adulting?

00:01:52.403 --> 00:01:55.616
And can you share some of
the challenges parents and

00:01:55.616 --> 00:01:58.618
kids face during
the transition to adulthood?

00:01:58.618 --> 00:01:59.650
&gt;&gt; Sure thing, well,

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.120
I guess I need to start by giving
a nod to the millennial generation.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.468
They're the ones who
coined the verb adulting.

00:02:06.468 --> 00:02:07.442
I don't want to adult.

00:02:07.442 --> 00:02:08.508
I don't know how to adult.

00:02:08.508 --> 00:02:10.174
Adulting is scary.

00:02:10.174 --> 00:02:13.956
And their anguish,
their angst over not knowing how or

00:02:13.956 --> 00:02:17.588
wanting to is really what
led me to write this thing.

00:02:17.588 --> 00:02:21.596
Then I had to examine what adulting meant,
and I came up with this as the definition.

00:02:21.596 --> 00:02:26.531
Adulting is simply the phase of
life between childhood and death,

00:02:26.531 --> 00:02:31.290
where you're not dependent upon
other people to plan, chart,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.918
fix, manage,
handle the stuff of life for you.

00:02:34.918 --> 00:02:37.597
Where you have this delicious freedom and
independence.

00:02:37.597 --> 00:02:42.869
And the terrifying knowledge that, hey,
it's kind of on me to figure this out,

00:02:42.869 --> 00:02:45.958
which is not to say any of
us ever has to go it alone.

00:02:45.958 --> 00:02:47.857
So that's what adulting is.

00:02:47.857 --> 00:02:51.934
And the challenges when it comes to
parents and kids is, as parents,

00:02:51.934 --> 00:02:55.940
we grew this child in our body perhaps,
or adopted into our family,

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.858
or our partner gave birth to it.

00:02:57.858 --> 00:03:02.708
And we are completely responsible
in those early months and years.

00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:07.608
But over the course of that child's life,
we're supposed to be slowly moving

00:03:07.608 --> 00:03:12.597
from being completely in charge of their
life to turning the reins over to them.

00:03:12.597 --> 00:03:16.162
And I think this big leap
from the end of high school,

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:20.943
the start of college, the start of work,
the start of military life,

00:03:20.943 --> 00:03:25.748
whatever awaits after high school is
to leap over a chasm in some ways.

00:03:25.748 --> 00:03:29.942
I mean, there's a sense that, my gosh,
there's a big gap I might fall into or

00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:31.016
I might not make it.

00:03:31.016 --> 00:03:32.741
And, of course, that's not literally true.

00:03:32.741 --> 00:03:34.921
But I think it's an important moment for

00:03:34.921 --> 00:03:38.770
us as parents to be preparing for,
to be thinking, okay, what do I want

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:42.838
to have confidence my kid can do when
they're no longer living in our home?

00:03:42.838 --> 00:03:46.284
When I'm no longer the primary one
responsible for their food, and

00:03:46.284 --> 00:03:49.138
their shelter, and
their day-to-day well-being?

00:03:49.138 --> 00:03:52.068
We want to be parenting
in the teenage years, and

00:03:52.068 --> 00:03:56.540
even earlier toward the inevitability
that we all want this young person

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.869
to be a free-standing adult who
can fend for themselves one day.

00:04:00.869 --> 00:04:03.110
&gt;&gt; And it is so fraught with challenges.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.538
I am going through this
now as a parent myself.

00:04:05.538 --> 00:04:10.414
It sounds like it's really balancing
between freedom and responsibility,

00:04:10.414 --> 00:04:12.897
both for parents, as well as for kids.

00:04:12.897 --> 00:04:16.846
What advice and guidance do you have for
helping parents and

00:04:16.846 --> 00:04:21.995
kids communicate about some of these
challenges in achieving this balance?

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:24.528
&gt;&gt; You're absolutely right that
it's freedom and responsibility.

00:04:24.528 --> 00:04:28.997
I can hear John Hennessey, who was
president when I last worked at Stanford,

00:04:28.997 --> 00:04:31.488
every year giving the graduation speech.

00:04:31.488 --> 00:04:36.437
And really helping the graduates
understand that with this freedom and

00:04:36.437 --> 00:04:39.168
independence comes responsibility.

00:04:39.168 --> 00:04:41.284
Responsibility isn't a bad thing.

00:04:41.284 --> 00:04:43.871
Responsibility is actually
an amazing thing.

00:04:43.871 --> 00:04:48.348
And I think we parents have to do
a better job of narrating to that truth.

00:04:48.348 --> 00:04:55.355
That is, you're going to be responsible
for more of your own things.

00:04:55.355 --> 00:04:58.758
You're going to be responsible for making
sure your homework is in its bag and

00:04:58.758 --> 00:04:59.577
taken to school.

00:04:59.577 --> 00:05:01.885
You're going to be responsible for
making your own lunch.

00:05:01.885 --> 00:05:04.967
You're going to be responsible for
making your own doctor's appointments.

00:05:04.967 --> 00:05:09.706
Conveying the expectation that these
things will be turned over to the kid, and

00:05:09.706 --> 00:05:11.488
that that's not a bad thing.

00:05:11.488 --> 00:05:15.510
You're actually trusting that
they either can already or

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.569
can almost handle that task.

00:05:17.569 --> 00:05:20.852
It's really by handing somebody
some additional responsibility,

00:05:20.852 --> 00:05:24.540
which we're supposed to be doing
increasingly over the years of childhood.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.290
We're actually signaling,
I believe you can.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:33.373
And that turns out, Matt, to be a really
important indicator of a person's

00:05:33.373 --> 00:05:38.790
wellness, the extent to which they believe
they can do the task in front of them.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.176
So a vote of confidence
from a parent saying, hey,

00:05:41.176 --> 00:05:43.241
you're going to be responsible for
this now.

00:05:43.241 --> 00:05:47.469
As long as it's done kindly as opposed to
feeling like your parent has turned your

00:05:47.469 --> 00:05:51.961
back on you, it's really quite empowering
to be given additional responsibility.

00:05:51.961 --> 00:05:53.770
And that's how the freedom and

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:56.908
the responsibility really
nicely then go together.

00:05:56.908 --> 00:06:01.495
&gt;&gt; So it sounds to me like part
of the challenge is to set clear

00:06:01.495 --> 00:06:06.371
expectations along the way, but
also to think about the tone

00:06:06.371 --> 00:06:10.402
in which you're communicating
those messages.

00:06:10.402 --> 00:06:15.221
So that as you're having these
conversations, you're being supportive,

00:06:15.221 --> 00:06:18.331
but also very clear about
what is happening now and

00:06:18.331 --> 00:06:21.014
will happen in the future, is that right?

00:06:21.014 --> 00:06:25.582
&gt;&gt; Exactly right, I mean, I think we're
at our best when we're parenting,

00:06:25.582 --> 00:06:30.370
when we're providing unconditional love,
a tremendous amount of empathy,

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.941
optimism that the kid is going
to be able to make their way.

00:06:33.941 --> 00:06:41.574
So the tone of voice, the body language
needs to be that of a compassionate,

00:06:41.574 --> 00:06:45.885
yet emotionally somewhat distant adult.

00:06:45.885 --> 00:06:48.478
And this may sound a bit odd to people.

00:06:48.478 --> 00:06:50.522
Aren't we parents supposed to
be very close to our kids?

00:06:50.522 --> 00:06:54.261
And kind of why would you advocate for
psychological distance?

00:06:54.261 --> 00:06:58.697
What I'm responding to is
the overparenting tendency, which says,

00:06:58.697 --> 00:07:00.809
your homework needs to get done.

00:07:00.809 --> 00:07:01.781
You need to get an A.

00:07:01.781 --> 00:07:05.350
You need to get into this college or
go off and do this career so

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:07.034
that I feel okay as a parent.

00:07:07.034 --> 00:07:11.639
That's the psychological intertwinedness
that's actually really unhealthy for

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:13.398
both the kid and for the parent.

00:07:13.398 --> 00:07:17.414
The more healthy distance is when you
can care very much about how they are,

00:07:17.414 --> 00:07:19.214
you can offer help and guidance.

00:07:19.214 --> 00:07:23.506
But you're not feeling that you own
every single thing that they do,

00:07:23.506 --> 00:07:28.985
whether it's their homework, or their
activities, or their college applications.

00:07:28.985 --> 00:07:33.743
We want to be in this place where our
child has the agency, the autonomy,

00:07:33.743 --> 00:07:37.567
to kind of make their own way and
have their experiences.

00:07:37.567 --> 00:07:41.726
Make their choices, fall a little bit
here and there, pick themselves up and

00:07:41.726 --> 00:07:44.168
be stronger based on
what they experienced.

00:07:44.168 --> 00:07:48.537
And our tone needs to be even and
positive, optimistic, kind and

00:07:48.537 --> 00:07:53.398
loving, but never nudging them like,
why aren't you doing it better?

00:07:53.398 --> 00:08:00.141
We expect better of you, and
certainly not being disinterested.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.206
It's sort of like, picture how loving,
you can be with your niece or nephew, or

00:08:06.206 --> 00:08:08.250
your best friend's kid.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:09.790
Where you care about the kid, but

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:13.145
you don't feel obligated to tell them
precisely what to do all the time,

00:08:13.145 --> 00:08:15.680
and you don't feel obligated
to solve their problem.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:19.701
That's the healthy psychological
distance I think we are going for

00:08:19.701 --> 00:08:21.049
actually as parents.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:27.016
&gt;&gt; I really like that image,
that will be very helpful to me, and

00:08:27.016 --> 00:08:29.344
hopefully many other folks.

00:08:29.344 --> 00:08:30.657
And I like how you highlighted it.

00:08:30.657 --> 00:08:33.452
It's not just about what you say but
how you say it,

00:08:33.452 --> 00:08:36.875
that non-verbal openness
is really critical.

00:08:36.875 --> 00:08:41.014
We have on this podcast talked about
improvisation in lots of different ways to

00:08:41.014 --> 00:08:42.832
help people be present oriented,

00:08:42.832 --> 00:08:46.645
to help people feel more
comfortable speaking spontaneously.

00:08:46.645 --> 00:08:51.070
You talk about adopting principles
from improv and design thinking,

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:53.095
to help when people feel stuck.

00:08:53.095 --> 00:08:56.745
Can you tell us more about this and
how this can help?

00:08:56.745 --> 00:09:01.765
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so a lot of people who
are trying to spread their wings and

00:09:01.765 --> 00:09:04.750
enter adult land feel stuck.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:06.729
And I think part of
that is the sense that,

00:09:06.729 --> 00:09:08.720
I have to know what I'm doing in advance.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:12.259
I have to be really good at it before I
even start, kind of a perfectionistic

00:09:12.259 --> 00:09:15.660
tendency, I have to know what I want,
I have to know who I am.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.286
And I think an overarching message
of this new book, Your Turn is, hey,

00:09:19.286 --> 00:09:22.686
you don't have to know,
you don't have to have it all figured out.

00:09:22.686 --> 00:09:25.776
The point is, just start to move
in the direction of something.

00:09:25.776 --> 00:09:30.834
And so both from the D school folks and
from improv more broadly,

00:09:30.834 --> 00:09:35.340
I borrowed the language bias
toward action is anywhere.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:39.939
And I think in whether you're a designer
or whether you're an improv actor

00:09:39.939 --> 00:09:44.070
trying to entertain an audience,
it's just all about let's go.

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:47.900
Let's just try something, let's,
this isn't about it has to be perfect.

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:53.473
This is just about what can happen when
we leave this point we're stuck in,

00:09:53.473 --> 00:09:58.612
or standing in our comfort zone perhaps,
or our stuck zone perhaps.

00:09:58.612 --> 00:10:01.719
And just take a step in any direction,
you will learn something,

00:10:01.719 --> 00:10:03.145
you will have an experience.

00:10:03.145 --> 00:10:09.620
You will analyze the data, how you felt,
what you might do differently,

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:14.536
and all of that begins to
create some velocity forward.

00:10:14.536 --> 00:10:17.093
&gt;&gt; The bias towards
action is such a powerful

00:10:17.093 --> 00:10:20.160
lesson from both design thinking and
improv, and

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:24.997
and it plays out in lots of ways in our
relationships and in our communication.

00:10:24.997 --> 00:10:29.660
There's a corollary to that notion which
is good is great, good enough is great,

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:33.322
and it's this notion that we put
a lot of pressure on ourselves.

00:10:33.322 --> 00:10:36.944
And one of the reasons we don't move
forward, is we're afraid it won't go

00:10:36.944 --> 00:10:39.430
the way we want or
it won't be as good as it could be.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:45.408
And just giving ourselves permission to
move forward is really, really powerful.

00:10:45.408 --> 00:10:47.458
In your book, Your Turn,

00:10:47.458 --> 00:10:52.490
you have a chapter entitled
start talking to strangers.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:56.983
Can you explain the advice you give in
this chapter, and share any breath best

00:10:56.983 --> 00:11:02.380
practices you have that can help people
use communication to connect with others?

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:06.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that chapter opens by
saying this, or in the first couple of

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:09.640
paragraphs, this may be the most
obvious point I make in the book, but

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.340
every single human is a stranger
to you at the outset.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:19.270
I'm deliberately subverting the childhood
norm, don't talk to strangers.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.868
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Young adults were raised with, and

00:11:21.868 --> 00:11:25.830
making the point that this
was an overbroad rule.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:30.560
The rule should have been learned, let me
teach you how to discern the one creepy

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:35.370
stranger from the vast majority of
humans who are perfectly fine child.

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:37.979
Let me let you walk
through the world with me,

00:11:37.979 --> 00:11:41.493
watching how I discern whether
a stranger is okay to talk to.

00:11:41.493 --> 00:11:46.456
Instead, we've given this overbroad rule
don't talk to strangers, and many, many,

00:11:46.456 --> 00:11:49.510
many, many young people
really don't know how to be.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:51.502
They feel afraid, they feel unsure,

00:11:51.502 --> 00:11:55.072
they feel something's wrong if
stranger is trying to talk to them.

00:11:55.072 --> 00:12:00.567
And that's boy, if there's anything
that's inhibited, or really prevented

00:12:00.567 --> 00:12:06.076
a normal free flowing conversation
between people who've not previously met.

00:12:06.076 --> 00:12:08.954
This mantra,
don't talk to strangers, maybe it.

00:12:08.954 --> 00:12:09.618
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:09.618 --> 00:12:15.580
&gt;&gt; Well, we shouldn't,
blanket rules rarely apply, right?

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:18.550
In the instinct about how
to approach somebody.

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:22.000
You're going to have to talk to strangers
when you leave your family home,

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.682
you're going to go to a college or
a community college, or

00:12:24.682 --> 00:12:26.292
the workplace with the military.

00:12:26.292 --> 00:12:28.950
There would be people who have
information that you need.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.520
There will be people who
can help you on your path.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:33.720
There will be people that you can help.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:38.192
And, all of those things are going to
require us opening our mouths and

00:12:38.192 --> 00:12:40.420
talking to this person.

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:44.746
But that's not all, I mean we've got to
be respectful toward this other person.

00:12:44.746 --> 00:12:48.144
We've got to also have a sense of
how to advocate for ourselves.

00:12:48.144 --> 00:12:52.069
Communication is such a delicate dance,
and

00:12:52.069 --> 00:12:57.243
kids need to emerge from
childhood having practiced that.

00:12:57.243 --> 00:13:02.094
Here's a fascinating thing that I end
up putting in the pages of Your Turn,

00:13:02.094 --> 00:13:06.414
that I got from psychologists and
Stanford alum Laurie Gottlieb.

00:13:06.414 --> 00:13:07.318
Because Julie so

00:13:07.318 --> 00:13:11.770
many young adults don't know how to
communicate with their significant others,

00:13:11.770 --> 00:13:15.523
or their boyfriend their girlfriend,
the person they're dating.

00:13:15.523 --> 00:13:20.180
And they show up in her therapy practice,
and they say I broke up with my boyfriend

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:24.661
we had an argument, and he wasn't 100%
there for me, so I just I dropped him.

00:13:24.661 --> 00:13:27.692
And she says Julie,
here's what I think is going on.

00:13:27.692 --> 00:13:31.955
Their childhood communication,
conversations were so regulated by well

00:13:31.955 --> 00:13:36.355
meaning parents, teachers, other adults
who sorted out difficulties when

00:13:36.355 --> 00:13:40.160
kids weren't getting along on
the playground, or on a play date.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:44.249
Who gave the kids language to use, to talk
to the other kids, not trusting the kids

00:13:44.249 --> 00:13:48.295
could ever figure this out themselves
without a little bit of guidance, right?

00:13:48.295 --> 00:13:50.480
A lot of adults did that handling.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:55.246
Now, we've got a set of young adults,
who haven't had that practice on

00:13:55.246 --> 00:14:00.000
the playground, in play dates,
in group play with their peers.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.546
And they think that when
someone loves them,

00:14:02.546 --> 00:14:05.237
it means I'll drop
everything to support you.

00:14:05.237 --> 00:14:07.149
Parents basically behave that way.

00:14:07.149 --> 00:14:07.855
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:14:07.855 --> 00:14:10.211
&gt;&gt; You're not having school,
I'll go argue with the teacher for you.

00:14:10.211 --> 00:14:14.134
And, instead of letting the student
practice, the child practice,

00:14:14.134 --> 00:14:19.001
how to have a difficult conversation with
somebody, so they become this young adult.

00:14:19.001 --> 00:14:23.682
Who doesn't have the skills to
communicate in what we would call

00:14:23.682 --> 00:14:25.768
run of the mill situations.

00:14:25.768 --> 00:14:29.150
A conversation with a friend,
a conversation with a lover,

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:31.553
a conversation with a colleague or a boss.

00:14:31.553 --> 00:14:36.444
We've got to let our kids, we need to
want for our kids to practice, practice,

00:14:36.444 --> 00:14:41.408
practice communicating with their fellow
humans well before they ever leave our

00:14:41.408 --> 00:14:44.560
homes, if they're to make
it in the world one day.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:49.993
&gt;&gt; The notion of practice and giving space
to learn how to communicate better is so

00:14:49.993 --> 00:14:52.562
important, and then helping them.

00:14:52.562 --> 00:14:57.070
I can imagine you would agree with this,
to reflect on that communication.

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.876
So you can learn from it and
do it differently or better,

00:14:59.876 --> 00:15:02.260
is also a valuable skill to add there.

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:03.470
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:07.308
&gt;&gt; So I'm going to ask you to get a little
meta here, Julie, if that's okay.

00:15:07.308 --> 00:15:10.248
I have seen you speak on
a number of occasions, and

00:15:10.248 --> 00:15:12.074
you are a phenomenal speaker.

00:15:12.074 --> 00:15:15.183
And an actually,
a great meeting facilitator as well, and

00:15:15.183 --> 00:15:17.800
and those two don't always go together.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.020
Can you share some of the things
you think about and do,

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:25.300
to prepare yourself and your content
before you present or facilitate?

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:31.538
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it feels a little personal,
but I'm not criticizing or critiquing,

00:15:31.538 --> 00:15:33.263
I'm just acknowledging.

00:15:33.263 --> 00:15:36.839
That I am going to tell you
that the honest truth, and

00:15:36.839 --> 00:15:40.010
honest to God truth here,
&gt;&gt; Please.

00:15:40.010 --> 00:15:42.451
&gt;&gt; I am spiritual but not religious.

00:15:42.451 --> 00:15:47.431
And, as a way to kind of get
my mindfulness game going,

00:15:47.431 --> 00:15:51.546
when I'm backstage about
to give a big talk,

00:15:51.546 --> 00:15:55.034
I offer what some might call a prayer.

00:15:55.034 --> 00:15:59.280
I am trying to summon my gratitude for
where I am and for what's about to happen.

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:05.431
And for something that's,
whatever's going on in my life,

00:16:05.431 --> 00:16:09.915
I then ask,
please let me have access to my intellect,

00:16:09.915 --> 00:16:13.150
to my sense of humor, to my intuition.

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:14.560
Let me connect.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:20.147
Let me help everybody here somehow feel
seen in the message I'm trying to deliver.

00:16:20.147 --> 00:16:22.148
I'm centering myself.

00:16:22.148 --> 00:16:26.012
I'm trying to create space
between wherever I just was,

00:16:26.012 --> 00:16:29.323
hurrying to get there if
I'm late pre pandemic.

00:16:29.323 --> 00:16:33.252
The traffic was bad or
I left too late or, I'm a little stressed,

00:16:33.252 --> 00:16:37.611
I just try to put a buffer between
everything that came up to that happened

00:16:37.611 --> 00:16:40.917
before that moment and
the moment that I'm entering.

00:16:40.917 --> 00:16:46.197
I'm trying to create a new container
which the conversation whether it's

00:16:46.197 --> 00:16:51.556
a speech to an audience or a conversation
among humans where I am just one of,

00:16:51.556 --> 00:16:54.780
I'm a fraction of the of the whole.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:17:00.394
I'm trying to be intentional about
what I need to do to create a safe and

00:17:00.394 --> 00:17:06.115
welcoming and interesting and
engaging conversational experience.

00:17:06.115 --> 00:17:09.525
&gt;&gt; Thank you first for
sharing that and second, so

00:17:09.525 --> 00:17:15.360
many things going on in that ritual
that you go through that are so helpful.

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:19.220
The ability to be present, and
getting yourself in the present moment.

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:22.553
Focusing through what you called prayer,
others called mantra,

00:17:22.553 --> 00:17:25.780
just focusing on the strengths
that you bring to the situation.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:30.378
Many of us feel so intimidated and think
about all the deficiencies we have before

00:17:30.378 --> 00:17:34.789
we communicate in front of others and
to focus on our strengths can only help.

00:17:34.789 --> 00:17:39.119
And to really think about the experience
I love that idea of the container that's

00:17:39.119 --> 00:17:42.636
being created and thinking about
that experience can only help.

00:17:42.636 --> 00:17:46.798
So many lessons to learn in
what you just shared and in so

00:17:46.798 --> 00:17:51.424
many lessons to learn in watching
you speak and facilitate.

00:17:51.424 --> 00:17:55.050
Before we end, I'd like to ask
you the same three questions,

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:57.790
I asked everyone who
joins me on this podcast.

00:17:57.790 --> 00:17:58.562
Are you up for that?

00:17:58.562 --> 00:17:59.898
&gt;&gt; I'm up for it, Matt.

00:17:59.898 --> 00:18:01.774
&gt;&gt; Excellent, number one,

00:18:01.774 --> 00:18:06.805
if you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received

00:18:06.805 --> 00:18:11.850
as a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would that be?

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:14.822
Stop using slides.

00:18:14.822 --> 00:18:19.515
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That might be the best
answer to that question given

00:18:19.515 --> 00:18:22.500
that I'm asking for a slide title.

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:23.890
Excellent, tell me more.

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:27.940
Tell me what slides,
what is it about slides that bother you?

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:30.961
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, I think some people
do manage to use slides quite

00:18:30.961 --> 00:18:35.101
artfully as a way to enhance whatever it
is they're doing with their body language.

00:18:35.101 --> 00:18:35.647
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:18:35.647 --> 00:18:40.248
&gt;&gt; And the actual language but too many of
us, no matter how many degrees you have or

00:18:40.248 --> 00:18:42.926
what role you play of
importance in the world,

00:18:42.926 --> 00:18:45.410
really rely on the slide
to tell the story.

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:50.721
To tell the experience people end up
glazing over they read your slide instead

00:18:50.721 --> 00:18:51.625
of reading you.

00:18:51.625 --> 00:18:52.269
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:18:52.269 --> 00:18:55.580
&gt;&gt; Look, my work is about connecting
with humans, humans to humans.

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:58.749
I actually am interested in
the energy that we can create.

00:18:58.749 --> 00:19:02.261
That exists in our bodies
that we can strengthen and

00:19:02.261 --> 00:19:05.456
raise, and share, and receive from others.

00:19:05.456 --> 00:19:09.711
So my work when I'm, this is what makes
the pandemic so hard for a speaker like

00:19:09.711 --> 00:19:13.777
me, I'm trying to create energy through
a Zoom experience and a webcam.

00:19:13.777 --> 00:19:18.866
But I'm really interested in like,
what can you feel radiating from

00:19:18.866 --> 00:19:24.478
the message that I am delivering and the
slide I think if I was to use slides and

00:19:24.478 --> 00:19:28.410
you can probably tell from
my answer that I don't.

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:33.670
I would just use imagery, some kind of
image to enhance rather than words.

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:35.540
I think it's too confusing.

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:38.827
&gt;&gt; Allow me to go to question number two,

00:19:38.827 --> 00:19:42.847
who is a communicator that you admire and
why?

00:19:42.847 --> 00:19:50.120
&gt;&gt; Oprah Winfrey has had a resurgence not
say Oprah wasn't ever always on our minds.

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:53.881
But she did this amazing interview
recently with Harry and Megan.

00:19:53.881 --> 00:19:54.819
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:19:54.819 --> 00:19:57.770
&gt;&gt; So we all billions of
people got to see her again.

00:19:57.770 --> 00:20:03.713
But I began watching Oprah I think she
her afternoon Chicago based talk show was

00:20:03.713 --> 00:20:10.030
on when I was either in late, my late high
school years certainly my college years.

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:13.345
She conveyed such a mad respect for
humans,

00:20:13.345 --> 00:20:18.730
a deep curiosity about the why behind
people's actions and language.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:23.505
And ultimately you could be having
a really tough conversation with somebody

00:20:23.505 --> 00:20:27.911
because the topic was tough or
because what they did was difficult bad,

00:20:27.911 --> 00:20:28.808
wrong, etc.

00:20:28.808 --> 00:20:35.857
Or concerning but she managed to elucidate
difficult subjects without judgment and

00:20:35.857 --> 00:20:41.118
I thought it was the most profound
intersection of curiosity,

00:20:41.118 --> 00:20:43.015
respect, and truth.

00:20:43.015 --> 00:20:46.750
I mean, Oprah was always
going to tell her own truth.

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:51.335
But you would feel even if she was
disagreeing with you, you would feel

00:20:51.335 --> 00:20:56.010
held in her arms as she did it, and
I just thought that was magnificent.

00:20:57.190 --> 00:21:00.203
&gt;&gt; I like the way you
dissected that curiosity,

00:21:00.203 --> 00:21:04.570
respect, and truth and
certainly she is an expert at all of that.

00:21:05.930 --> 00:21:14.283
What are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:21:14.283 --> 00:21:19.610
&gt;&gt; Maya Angelou, I believe said, they're
not going to remember what you said.

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:23.350
They're going to remember how
they felt when you said it.

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:27.859
Matt, for 10 years I had the distinct,
humbling joy of being the dean

00:21:27.859 --> 00:21:31.465
of freshmen on the Stanford campus and
I got a big speech,

00:21:31.465 --> 00:21:35.853
many big speeches in a year but
orientation is what I'm thinking of.

00:21:35.853 --> 00:21:40.572
And in the noise of orientation
200 events across six or

00:21:40.572 --> 00:21:45.881
seven days, I knew whatever I said
was not going to be memorable.

00:21:45.881 --> 00:21:47.663
They were drinking from a firehose, right?

00:21:47.663 --> 00:21:48.321
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:21:48.321 --> 00:21:52.003
&gt;&gt; Just trying not to get wet,
just try not to drown and yet

00:21:52.003 --> 00:21:56.809
we wanted some of that water to land
because it would be nourishing and so

00:21:56.809 --> 00:21:59.551
I aimed to convey that feeling, right?

00:21:59.551 --> 00:22:04.277
You will probably not remember the words,
but hopefully,

00:22:04.277 --> 00:22:08.530
you are having an experience
of being respected, and

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:12.990
helped ,and aided, and
cared about, and routed for.

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:18.122
So, I think that's the advice,
yes you're going to use words, but

00:22:18.122 --> 00:22:21.179
what is it ultimately that
you hope the recipient,

00:22:21.179 --> 00:22:23.970
the listener,
the reader is going take away?

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:30.298
My new book Your Turn, the narrative voice
of that took me three years to find and

00:22:30.298 --> 00:22:35.772
And it ultimately is trying to be me,
is me trying to be this compassionate,

00:22:35.772 --> 00:22:41.702
frank, older person saying, I know
you're terrified, I get that it's valid.

00:22:41.702 --> 00:22:46.370
And the thing is,
you gotta step into the adult place.

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:47.983
And I'm here rooting for you and

00:22:47.983 --> 00:22:51.840
I'm going to be alongside you on these
pages and you're going to be okay.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:54.380
Yes, you will.

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:56.404
And to try to do that on the page, I mean,

00:22:56.404 --> 00:22:59.036
readers will have to let me
know did I succeed or not?

00:22:59.036 --> 00:23:02.160
And maybe in some chapters I did and
others I didn't.

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:07.078
But that's it's trying to imagine
how the other person is going

00:23:07.078 --> 00:23:11.150
to receive any of the stuff
you have to offer.

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:14.572
And instead of focusing too much on
who am I, and how am I, what do I,

00:23:14.572 --> 00:23:16.630
think about it from their perspective.

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:19.420
It'll take some of the pressure off you,
I think.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:23.670
And it allows you to create empathy for
your reader, your listener, your audience.

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:27.810
And ultimately, that's going to lead to a
great presentation, a great communication.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:31.958
&gt;&gt; The notion of focusing
on your audience and

00:23:31.958 --> 00:23:37.490
what they need is a recurrent theme that
we hear across these podcast episodes.

00:23:37.490 --> 00:23:41.399
What you've added to it around
this notion of really thinking

00:23:41.399 --> 00:23:45.470
about the experience and
the feeling is very important as well.

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:52.994
Thank you for all of your time and
your insights, your ideas about support,

00:23:52.994 --> 00:23:59.900
compassion, both for ourselves and for the
young adults we're talking to is critical.

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:04.705
And these lessons extend well beyond
transitioning into adulthood.

00:24:04.705 --> 00:24:10.304
I encourage everyone to take time to read
Julie's books and to watch her talks.

00:24:10.304 --> 00:24:11.960
Thank you so much Julie.

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:15.095
&gt;&gt; Matt, thanks for having me and
to all the listeners thanks for

00:24:15.095 --> 00:24:17.458
spending time with me
today I appreciate you.

00:24:17.458 --> 00:24:20.635
&gt;&gt; Thank you for
listening to Think Fast Talk Smart,

00:24:20.635 --> 00:24:25.380
the podcast, a production of
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:29.790
To learn more, go to gsb.stanford.edu.

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:34.415
Please download other episodes
wherever you find your podcasts.

00:24:34.415 --> 00:24:38.345
